1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
It's an industry Paul Bunyan could have never imagined.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:09,000
Teaming He-Man and high-tech.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:12,000
Saws and software.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Blades and boards.

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:19,000
Each cry of timber evokes memories of a wild and woolly past.

6
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:23,000
And each tree that falls fuels debate about its future.

7
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Now, Logging Tech on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
The roar of machinery and crackling of timber scream it louder than any statistic.

9
00:00:45,820 --> 00:00:48,520
The world has a ravenous appetite for wood.

10
00:00:49,260 --> 00:00:54,040
Each year, loggers topple 4 billion trees to satisfy our insatiable demand.

11
00:00:54,041 --> 00:01:04,780
We devour trees for lumber, paper, and byproducts from aspirin to film to toothpaste.

12
00:01:04,781 --> 00:01:12,540
In the last 50 years, global demand has doubled, and Americans lead the way, consuming 40% of

13
00:01:12,541 --> 00:01:16,040
the forest's resources.

14
00:01:16,041 --> 00:01:19,040
Trying to answer the challenge is a new generation of loggers.

15
00:01:19,041 --> 00:01:21,040
I've grown up in the woods, really.

16
00:01:21,041 --> 00:01:26,040
I've spent many, many days in the woods with my dad and my grandfather.

17
00:01:26,041 --> 00:01:31,040
My grandfather had to be physically tough to go out every day and deal with the dangers

18
00:01:31,041 --> 00:01:36,040
of falling large timber and getting the production he had to get.

19
00:01:36,041 --> 00:01:43,040
Today, equipment makes our job out here relatively easy, but I would hope that my grandfather

20
00:01:43,041 --> 00:01:48,040
would be as proud of the job that I do today as I am of what he did in his era.

21
00:01:49,040 --> 00:01:58,540
The logging industry is characterized by people I would call geniuses with calloused hands,

22
00:01:58,700 --> 00:02:05,460
because they had to use the muscle that they had in their bodies to be able to invent ways to move

23
00:02:05,461 --> 00:02:10,860
logs that may weigh thousands and even tens of thousands of pounds around in the forest.

24
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:17,860
A single tree's journey from stump to sawmill seems to be a model of efficiency.

25
00:02:17,861 --> 00:02:21,860
It begins not in the forest, but at a computer.

26
00:02:21,861 --> 00:02:25,860
Specialized software helps foresters target groves to be harvested.

27
00:02:25,861 --> 00:02:29,860
Basically the software has a database in it

28
00:02:29,861 --> 00:02:33,860
in which people have actually gone out to the woods and collected information about

29
00:02:33,861 --> 00:02:37,860
the stands of trees, like the age of them, their species,

30
00:02:37,861 --> 00:02:41,860
or the trees per acre in the stand. And you can say, I'm interested in

31
00:02:41,861 --> 00:02:45,860
all stands of trees that are 60 years old. And it'll go through and bring up all

32
00:02:45,861 --> 00:02:49,040
of those stands for you so you can make the decisions about which stands you want

33
00:02:49,041 --> 00:02:52,860
to harvest based on what the computers pulled up for you.

34
00:02:52,861 --> 00:02:57,540
A tree is quick work for the tool that revolutionized the industry in the 20th

35
00:02:57,541 --> 00:03:01,540
century, the chainsaw.

36
00:03:01,541 --> 00:03:12,060
Trees that once took several minutes to fell now topple in seconds.

37
00:03:12,061 --> 00:03:21,520
But its productivity pales in comparison with this mechanical titan, the single grip harvester.

38
00:03:21,521 --> 00:03:27,060
Operators maneuver a powerful articulated arm tipped with a rotating head.

39
00:03:27,061 --> 00:03:29,740
Vice-like grapples take hold of the tree.

40
00:03:29,741 --> 00:03:36,680
Then a chainsaw shears it off at the base.

41
00:03:36,681 --> 00:03:41,380
It then propels the tree through scraping blades that delimit.

42
00:03:41,381 --> 00:03:46,160
At the same time, it carves it into precisely sawn logs, a process called bucking.

43
00:03:50,420 --> 00:03:55,900
The actual grapples that grab the tree are also knives.

44
00:03:56,320 --> 00:04:01,460
And on the head, there's two wheels that actually suck the tree through the head.

45
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:08,420
It'd be similar as taking a stick of straw with the seed still on top of it and just pulling it through your fingers.

46
00:04:08,421 --> 00:04:11,800
It works on the same concept as that.

47
00:04:12,680 --> 00:04:16,940
The saw blade is so powerful that few operators could control it by hand.

48
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:21,120
Conventional chainsaws have teeth on every other link in the chain.

49
00:04:22,580 --> 00:04:25,320
A harvester's blade has a tooth on every link.

50
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:31,480
This enables it to saw more rapidly through even the hardest wood.

51
00:04:32,780 --> 00:04:34,420
With the mechanized harvesting operations,

52
00:04:34,421 --> 00:04:39,020
operations, we can cut down trees 10 times faster than we can with a chainsaw.

53
00:04:39,021 --> 00:04:40,140
And we might cut 70

54
00:04:40,141 --> 00:04:46,020
trees in a day with a chainsaw, and we might cut 700 with a mechanized harvesting machine.

55
00:04:47,080 --> 00:04:57,600
Most I've ever done in one day is 1,250 trees, and my record per hour is 172 trees.

56
00:04:57,601 --> 00:05:06,240
felling and bucking timber is one thing moving is another when the terrain permits

57
00:05:06,241 --> 00:05:10,440
vehicles called forwarders shuttle logs to waiting transport trucks

58
00:05:10,441 --> 00:05:19,020
in more rugged country vehicles simply drag the logs loggers call it skidding

59
00:05:19,021 --> 00:05:25,440
a variation high lead skidding elevates the front end of a log from a suspended cable

60
00:05:25,441 --> 00:05:30,440
It begins and ends with crewmen called choker setters.

61
00:05:30,441 --> 00:05:37,440
Choker setting is the process of hooking the steel cables that yard logs to the landing.

62
00:05:37,441 --> 00:05:46,440
That choker is basically a steel noose that you put around the log and as it tightens up it squeezes the log and that's how it is yarded or pulled to the landing.

63
00:05:46,441 --> 00:05:50,440
The process is as perilous as it is effective.

64
00:05:50,441 --> 00:05:58,880
People working as choker setters today are faced with a variety of hazards.

65
00:05:58,881 --> 00:06:04,080
Cables could break, logs could fall, limbs could come out of the trees that were broken

66
00:06:04,081 --> 00:06:08,100
by the movement of the log up the cable corridor.

67
00:06:08,101 --> 00:06:12,380
Just the movement on that steep terrain is difficult.

68
00:06:12,381 --> 00:06:14,280
The communication is crucial.

69
00:06:14,281 --> 00:06:18,880
The lines don't move until a person with a whistle gets into a position in the clear

70
00:06:18,881 --> 00:06:23,980
and provides the signal to the yarder operator

71
00:06:23,981 --> 00:06:26,420
to move the lines.

72
00:06:30,160 --> 00:06:32,020
The worst situation you could be in,

73
00:06:32,520 --> 00:06:35,880
you're not expecting the lines to move, and they do,

74
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,220
and you're in a position where you're going to be in the way.

75
00:06:38,700 --> 00:06:41,980
You're talking about logs that weigh tens of thousands of pounds.

76
00:06:42,420 --> 00:06:44,040
There are no odds on that.

77
00:06:45,160 --> 00:06:46,520
It's you lose.

78
00:06:46,521 --> 00:06:49,194
Today's men get no break.

79
00:06:49,195 --> 00:06:55,720
Things are moving so fast that they're constantly on the run.

80
00:06:55,721 --> 00:06:58,981
The machinery's faster, the rigging's faster.

81
00:06:58,982 --> 00:07:02,460
So today's logging inherently is more dangerous,

82
00:07:02,461 --> 00:07:08,400
except we're not allowing it to be. We're saying safety has to be first.

83
00:07:09,520 --> 00:07:13,915
One of logging's latest innovations is a radio-controlled choker.

84
00:07:13,916 --> 00:07:15,200
It relieves crewmen

85
00:07:15,201 --> 00:07:19,100
have the perilous task of releasing the nooses.

86
00:07:19,101 --> 00:07:21,260
Releasing them manually is dangerous

87
00:07:21,261 --> 00:07:23,960
because it is at that moment when logs are most likely

88
00:07:23,961 --> 00:07:26,960
to suddenly shift or fall.

89
00:07:26,961 --> 00:07:30,560
Inside this choker bale, there's a battery, electric motor,

90
00:07:30,561 --> 00:07:32,320
a receiver, and a circuit board.

91
00:07:32,321 --> 00:07:34,760
The way the choker setter sets it is you'll

92
00:07:34,761 --> 00:07:37,040
notice there's a button here.

93
00:07:37,041 --> 00:07:42,460
He pushes this button out like that, takes his choker,

94
00:07:42,461 --> 00:07:47,140
puts it around the log into that slot and closes that bell.

95
00:07:47,660 --> 00:07:49,020
Now it's locked into position.

96
00:07:50,440 --> 00:07:52,380
So the log will come into the landing.

97
00:07:52,580 --> 00:07:53,360
They'll let it down.

98
00:07:54,260 --> 00:07:58,040
The operator will push a button and it will release this choker.

99
00:07:59,680 --> 00:08:02,200
No man has to get into that danger zone.

100
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:09,200
Logging's cutting-edge technology takes its most literal form in the sawmill.

101
00:08:12,460 --> 00:08:16,460
Georgia Pacific's mill in Coos Bay, Oregon, built in 1994,

102
00:08:16,461 --> 00:08:20,460
is one of the most productive in the United States.

103
00:08:20,461 --> 00:08:24,460
It takes about 100 log trucks a day to keep up with the sawmill.

104
00:08:24,461 --> 00:08:28,460
Non-annual basis for producing about 200 million feet of lumber.

105
00:08:28,461 --> 00:08:32,460
That's enough to build about 17,000 average single-family homes.

106
00:08:32,461 --> 00:08:36,460
Or if you want to look at it another way, enough lumber to make a 2x4

107
00:08:36,461 --> 00:08:40,460
that would stretch around the world two times.

108
00:08:42,460 --> 00:08:46,660
A log's first encounter here is with the debarker.

109
00:08:47,340 --> 00:08:52,040
The bark must be scraped off because it is encased with dirt that can dull the saws that lie ahead.

110
00:08:55,320 --> 00:08:59,360
Debarked logs then race by conveyor before the vigilant eyes of the sawyer.

111
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:06,680
He mans a computerized scanner that has only a fleeting instant to formulate the most efficient cutting strategy for each piece of timber.

112
00:09:06,681 --> 00:09:15,260
on an average day in our sawmill we're going to run 4 000 logs through the head rig or in this

113
00:09:15,261 --> 00:09:20,160
case which we call the chippen saw to have a person try and make a decision and get the most

114
00:09:20,161 --> 00:09:26,760
value out of that log 4 000 times a day without making a mistake just doesn't work so today's

115
00:09:26,761 --> 00:09:33,860
modern sawmill uses a lot of computers a lot of scanners as the log comes through here i'm looking

116
00:09:33,861 --> 00:09:39,640
at it and I'm looking at the scan that the computer's given, it can see everything, knots,

117
00:09:39,820 --> 00:09:44,480
taper, and then it basically immediately, as soon as it clears the scanner, it's made

118
00:09:44,481 --> 00:09:50,220
a decision and it pops up a picture and it gives the length, the diameter, every board

119
00:09:50,221 --> 00:09:51,000
that's in there.

120
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:55,240
And usually the scanner does a really good solution.

121
00:09:55,860 --> 00:09:58,960
Two kinds of blades transform logs into lumber.

122
00:09:58,961 --> 00:10:06,620
A bandsaw, a loop of steel strung tight between two pulleys, squares off logs into more manageable units called cants.

123
00:10:08,480 --> 00:10:14,200
Down the line, a gang of circular saws carves the cants in one pass into separate boards.

124
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:27,060
The blades are brazed with carbide, a hard carbon compound that helps the teeth stay sharper longer.

125
00:10:28,960 --> 00:10:33,240
But the real key to a saw's efficiency is its thickness, or kerf.

126
00:10:34,140 --> 00:10:36,180
Thin blades produce the least sawdust.

127
00:10:37,220 --> 00:10:41,680
20 years ago, 30 years ago, saw kerf, which is the thickness of the saw,

128
00:10:42,040 --> 00:10:45,440
could have been as much as a quarter of an inch, or 250 thousandths.

129
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:46,880
That's the same as a quarter of an inch.

130
00:10:47,480 --> 00:10:51,280
Today, I can't tell you specifics because it's kind of one of our secrets

131
00:10:51,281 --> 00:10:53,920
that we're significantly less than half of that.

132
00:10:53,921 --> 00:10:59,800
If you were to use the old circle saws, you might get 10 pieces of lumber out of it.

133
00:10:59,801 --> 00:11:03,680
Now with the technology and the improvements, you may get 13 or 14 pieces of lumber out

134
00:11:03,681 --> 00:11:04,680
of it.

135
00:11:04,681 --> 00:11:12,740
You've got that much more of that log going into lumber instead of sawdust.

136
00:11:12,741 --> 00:11:17,280
Cut lumber finally makes its way to a crew of graders who inspect each piece to determine

137
00:11:17,281 --> 00:11:18,280
its quality.

138
00:11:18,281 --> 00:11:27,698
They have just a second, maybe a second and a half to look at each piece and apply the grade to that piece according to the characteristics that they're viewing at the

139
00:11:27,699 --> 00:11:28,399
time.

140
00:11:29,260 --> 00:11:33,640
We have a special crayon that the graders use that are readable by this system.

141
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:41,840
As it passes underneath the camera lens, it takes a photograph of that, and it will sort the lumber out based on the mark that graders have put on the lumber.

142
00:11:41,841 --> 00:11:50,140
lumber bound for market is used to make everything from furniture to crates to toys

143
00:11:50,141 --> 00:11:57,020
but most of it ends up here the construction industry consumes every other board produced

144
00:11:57,021 --> 00:12:03,520
in the united states a nation with a voracious appetite for wood is using it to grow

145
00:12:03,521 --> 00:12:09,820
feeding that appetite is a challenge as old as america itself

146
00:12:09,821 --> 00:12:15,900
the hardy men who started it all forged a legend as large as the timber they fell

147
00:12:15,901 --> 00:12:25,100
wood abounds in medicine cabinets pills in tablet form are held together with lignin

148
00:12:25,101 --> 00:12:31,980
a glue extracted from trees adhesive on bandages is made from sap toothpaste owes its consistency

149
00:12:31,981 --> 00:12:34,380
to cellulose, a natural wood polymer.

