152
00:12:44,320 --> 00:12:45,900
Two, one, go!

153
00:12:47,580 --> 00:12:51,160
Just as the rodeo pays homage to the romantic image of the cowboy,

154
00:12:52,440 --> 00:12:55,900
timber sports celebrate the larger-than-life persona of the lumberjack.

155
00:12:55,901 --> 00:13:02,900
Although 21st century loggers embrace the advantages of mechanization, they revel in their roots.

156
00:13:02,901 --> 00:13:08,900
Those roots date back to the beginning of the last ice age.

157
00:13:08,901 --> 00:13:15,900
In a moment of inspiration, an ancient innovator sought to improve the hand-held sharpened stone he used as a cutting tool.

158
00:13:15,901 --> 00:13:24,900
He attached it to a long wooden handle, creating logging's most time-honored tool, the axe.

159
00:13:24,901 --> 00:13:25,700
the axe.

160
00:13:25,701 --> 00:13:33,900
In the centuries ahead, bronze replaced stone, eventually empowering the Egyptians

161
00:13:33,901 --> 00:13:38,400
to complete history's oldest known logging expedition.

162
00:13:38,401 --> 00:13:40,900
In 2700 BC, they harvested the

163
00:13:40,901 --> 00:13:44,400
lush cedar forests that covered much of Lebanon.

164
00:13:44,401 --> 00:13:47,900
Today, only a few scattered cedars remain there.

165
00:13:47,901 --> 00:13:53,960
remain there. Tools and techniques advanced little in the centuries that

166
00:13:53,961 --> 00:14:00,440
followed. In every culture the essentials were the same, an axe, a strong back and a

167
00:14:00,441 --> 00:14:06,560
river to transport the heavy timber. For the next two millennia loggers feasted on

168
00:14:06,561 --> 00:14:12,260
the timberlands, especially in Europe. But until the 14th century woodworkers sawed

169
00:14:12,261 --> 00:14:13,983
logs strictly by hand.

170
00:14:13,984 --> 00:14:19,620
Then water wheels began powering the first sawmills in Germany, followed

171
00:14:19,621 --> 00:14:22,906
by windmill-powered systems in Holland.

172
00:14:22,907 --> 00:14:27,540
Forests fell ever faster as the population rose. Today,

173
00:14:27,541 --> 00:14:31,319
only 3% of Europe's ancient woodlands remained.

174
00:14:31,320 --> 00:14:35,500
In the New World, old techniques enabled lumberjacks

175
00:14:35,501 --> 00:14:39,505
to successfully assail the forests of New England and the Great Lakes.

176
00:14:39,506 --> 00:14:40,420
But when loggers

177
00:14:40,421 --> 00:14:45,502
ventured west in the 1850s, time-honored methods proved woefully inadequate.

178
00:14:45,503 --> 00:14:47,040
They were thunderstruck

179
00:14:47,041 --> 00:14:52,033
as they stepped in the shadows of the largest trees on the planet.

180
00:14:52,034 --> 00:14:54,000
Douglas fir, Sitka spruce,

181
00:14:54,660 --> 00:14:59,060
coast redwoods, and giant sequoias dwarfed the white pines abundant in the east.

182
00:14:59,960 --> 00:15:03,080
The attitude of the logger who came out here was one of all.

183
00:15:03,081 --> 00:15:04,640
The average tree in the forest

184
00:15:04,641 --> 00:15:09,516
of Maine was about, a big tree was 100 feet high, maybe two or three feet thick.

185
00:15:09,517 --> 00:15:10,217
When

186
00:15:10,218 --> 00:15:13,220
When they got out here they found in the case of the giant sequoias they were looking at

187
00:15:13,221 --> 00:15:17,840
a tree 230 feet high in some instances, 30 feet thick.

188
00:15:17,841 --> 00:15:22,480
So when the loggers came west they had no idea what they were getting into.

189
00:15:22,481 --> 00:15:27,820
Imagine somebody coming up to a tree 30 feet across and 230 feet high with a handsaw and

190
00:15:27,821 --> 00:15:29,820
an axe.

191
00:15:29,821 --> 00:15:35,880
Lumberjacks cast aside their six foot saws and forged models up to 22 feet long.

192
00:15:35,881 --> 00:15:40,460
The men trying to harvest the treasure of timber were a motley throng of wayward settlers,

193
00:15:40,461 --> 00:15:43,820
ship deserters and frustrated forty-niners.

194
00:15:43,821 --> 00:15:48,820
But these misfits became bonded by a common purpose, a lust for adventure and a freewheeling

195
00:15:48,821 --> 00:15:52,160
life in one of nature's grandest arenas.

196
00:15:52,161 --> 00:15:55,200
They were a pretty hardy bunch, you know, they were willing to fight at the drop of a

197
00:15:55,201 --> 00:15:59,200
hat and of course they were in marvelous shape, lean and not too big.

198
00:15:59,201 --> 00:16:03,000
The average guy who worked in the woods was about five foot seven or eight, maybe 150

199
00:16:03,001 --> 00:16:04,000
pounds.

200
00:16:04,001 --> 00:16:09,160
in a while you'd find a big guy, 6'4", 220, but on the whole they were pretty lean, wiry

201
00:16:09,161 --> 00:16:10,160
group.

202
00:16:10,161 --> 00:16:15,440
One of the first obstacles loggers faced was the nearly impenetrable swollen bases

203
00:16:15,441 --> 00:16:18,020
of the mammoth trees.

204
00:16:18,021 --> 00:16:23,460
All the resins from years and years start to congregate in the last six, eight feet

205
00:16:23,461 --> 00:16:25,940
of the trunk of the tree, right above the stump.

206
00:16:25,941 --> 00:16:30,780
And so what they did is they had to get above that butt swell.

207
00:16:30,781 --> 00:16:36,380
The solution, called the springboard, became standard practice among lumberjacks well into the 20th century.

208
00:16:36,920 --> 00:16:41,280
Springboard is really nothing more than about a six or eight inch wide board.

209
00:16:41,760 --> 00:16:44,480
It had a little hook on the end and that would catch.

210
00:16:44,880 --> 00:16:48,780
And you could hop up and you could put a 250 pound man on this springboard.

211
00:16:50,460 --> 00:16:56,500
Springboards gave lumberjacks just the boost they needed to begin the laborious process of bringing the mighty trees down.

212
00:16:56,501 --> 00:17:02,000
They would come in and decide where their horizontal undercut was going to be.

213
00:17:02,001 --> 00:17:08,000
And then they would cut across the tree to a depth maybe about a third or so of the tree diameter.

214
00:17:08,001 --> 00:17:11,500
They would then come in and chop out the undercut.

215
00:17:11,501 --> 00:17:17,000
After the tree is faced up, we call it, they would come around behind the tree

216
00:17:17,001 --> 00:17:22,500
and then begin sawing at another horizontal cut a little bit above the undercut.

217
00:17:22,501 --> 00:17:29,180
and then they'll have to leave a hinge between the front cut or the undercut and the back cut

218
00:17:29,181 --> 00:17:31,480
to guide the tree in the direction that they want it to go.

219
00:17:35,780 --> 00:17:41,660
Lumberjacks would even put out stakes or markers out in the area where they were going to drop the tree

220
00:17:41,661 --> 00:17:45,560
and they'd take bets on whether or not they could drive a stake in the ground with a tree.

221
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:49,800
As the trees fell, their titanic limbs often snapped off

222
00:17:49,801 --> 00:17:52,640
and lodged in the treetops towering above the forest floor.

223
00:17:53,520 --> 00:17:57,280
Days, even weeks later, they could plummet toward unsuspecting loggers.

224
00:17:58,280 --> 00:18:01,900
Well, if limbs broke out, especially the limb from, say, 100 feet up,

225
00:18:02,020 --> 00:18:05,220
and came hustling down, that's what we called a widow maker.

226
00:18:05,760 --> 00:18:07,640
Because that fell, and if that hit him on the head,

227
00:18:07,880 --> 00:18:09,940
I mean, he would just drive him in the ground.

228
00:18:10,440 --> 00:18:12,660
You know, and if you were a married lumberjack, you know,

229
00:18:12,661 --> 00:18:15,760
you've just, your widow is going to a funeral now.

230
00:18:15,761 --> 00:18:22,260
Every tree that toppled set in motion the lumberjack's colorful supporting cast, assigned

231
00:18:22,261 --> 00:18:25,860
the Herculean task of transporting the huge logs.

232
00:18:25,861 --> 00:18:31,860
Their destination was a nearby river, the critical link to civilization around which

233
00:18:31,861 --> 00:18:35,740
every logging camp was based.

234
00:18:35,741 --> 00:18:38,000
First came the bullwhackers.

235
00:18:38,001 --> 00:18:43,560
For decades, their teams of oxen dragged timber by steel chains over a path of half-buried

236
00:18:43,561 --> 00:18:51,000
logs called the skid road at the end of the skid road were the sure-footed river pigs

237
00:18:52,120 --> 00:18:57,800
for the next century they navigated america's waterways driving logs as far as 70 miles to the

238
00:18:57,801 --> 00:19:04,280
sawmills each came equipped only with spiked boots and a pole fitted with a hook designed

239
00:19:04,281 --> 00:19:10,040
to maneuver the floating timber fighting a constant battle to break up log jams in the

240
00:19:10,041 --> 00:19:12,440
the congested rivers, every river pig

241
00:19:12,441 --> 00:19:16,220
knew that one misstep could be fatal.

242
00:19:16,221 --> 00:19:18,720
Imagine trying to stand on a log

243
00:19:18,721 --> 00:19:22,460
or moving from one log to another on a river.

244
00:19:22,461 --> 00:19:25,140
And if you fell off of those logs into the water,

245
00:19:25,141 --> 00:19:27,140
and the water was filled with logs,

246
00:19:27,141 --> 00:19:29,060
you might not come up because you might not

247
00:19:29,061 --> 00:19:31,240
have a place to come up.

248
00:19:31,241 --> 00:19:35,220
I could just imagine that feeling of trying to find daylight,

249
00:19:35,221 --> 00:19:36,320
trying to look up.

250
00:19:36,321 --> 00:19:38,520
And all you could see above you was a wall of logs

251
00:19:38,521 --> 00:19:40,520
and just going, this is not good.

252
00:19:41,560 --> 00:19:43,880
After hours, the river pigs and their colleagues

253
00:19:43,881 --> 00:19:46,800
brought their bravado indoors to the crowded bunkhouses.

254
00:19:47,540 --> 00:19:50,980
Up to 40 shared one room, reeking of sweat, tobacco,

255
00:19:51,200 --> 00:19:54,280
drying socks, and overflowing spittoons.

256
00:19:56,240 --> 00:19:59,000
A more popular venue was the camp's dining hall.

257
00:20:00,040 --> 00:20:03,160
The main event for loggers was food.

258
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,840
The average logger burned 8,000 calories a day

259
00:20:06,841 --> 00:20:09,560
because his work was so hard he never got fat.

260
00:20:10,780 --> 00:20:13,660
The loggers' appetites, however, weren't restricted to food.

261
00:20:14,680 --> 00:20:18,480
Many sought release from their perilous profession in brothels and saloons

262
00:20:18,481 --> 00:20:21,060
that sprung up like weeds around their camp.

263
00:20:22,360 --> 00:20:25,240
After blowing four months' salary and one night of debauchery,

264
00:20:25,680 --> 00:20:28,640
they returned to the forests to forge their place in history.

265
00:20:31,400 --> 00:20:33,920
They had little inkling that in years to come,

266
00:20:33,921 --> 00:20:38,320
New machines and daring technologies would transform their world.

267
00:20:40,400 --> 00:20:47,540
The term skid row takes its name from the rundown saloons and boarding houses near the skid roads of western logging camps.

268
00:20:49,080 --> 00:20:51,200
Logging Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

269
00:20:54,880 --> 00:20:58,220
We now return to Logging Tech on Modern Marvels.

270
00:20:58,221 --> 00:21:06,700
Beginning in the 1870s, timbermen in the remote western forests heard the shrill echo of progress.

271
00:21:08,760 --> 00:21:16,000
The first transcontinental trains linked their vast virgin woodlands with eastern markets, whose own forests had been depleted.

272
00:21:17,360 --> 00:21:23,400
Demand soared, but the industrial revolution that drove the railroad west also empowered the loggers.

273
00:21:23,401 --> 00:21:32,180
mechanization radically changed how timber was moved for decades to come a huffing monstrosity

274
00:21:32,181 --> 00:21:39,080
called a donkey engine was the nucleus of every logging operation powered by steam it turned a

275
00:21:39,081 --> 00:21:46,140
capstan that could pull cable drawn logs more powerfully than any animal the donkey name came

276
00:21:46,141 --> 00:21:51,180
about because it was such a small machine people said it really didn't have enough horsepower so

277
00:21:51,181 --> 00:21:53,820
they couldn't call it a horse, so they named it the donkey instead.

278
00:21:54,840 --> 00:21:56,960
Donkey engines packed enough power, however,

279
00:21:56,961 --> 00:22:00,300
to inspire the high-lead skidding technique still in use today.

280
00:22:01,720 --> 00:22:06,300
A donkey's tow line was wound over a pulley atop what was called a spar tree.

281
00:22:07,200 --> 00:22:11,400
The increased leverage enabled it to pull logs up to 2,000 feet away,

282
00:22:11,780 --> 00:22:13,500
with their head ends high in the air,

283
00:22:13,880 --> 00:22:16,080
riding free above the stumps and underbrush.

284
00:22:17,040 --> 00:22:19,360
Moving logs when they're totally on the ground

285
00:22:19,361 --> 00:22:23,220
takes a lot more energy than if you have one end of the log lifted.

286
00:22:23,760 --> 00:22:29,520
Cables were elevated so that you would get at least one end suspension of the log above the ground

287
00:22:29,521 --> 00:22:32,460
to reduce the amount of energy required to pull those logs in.

288
00:22:34,280 --> 00:22:37,400
Productivity soared, but so did the accident rate.

289
00:22:38,420 --> 00:22:40,780
One of the biggest hazards was the bite of the line.

290
00:22:40,980 --> 00:22:44,120
That's B-I-G-H-T, not B-I-T-E, the bite of the line.

291
00:22:44,121 --> 00:22:50,480
the donkey engine would start to pull that cable up and it would pull tight if you were caught in

292
00:22:50,481 --> 00:22:57,920
the bite of the line with a foot or an ankle that was the end of your legs as the new system forced

293
00:22:57,921 --> 00:23:03,200
the bullwhackers out of a job it created a spectacular new specialist the high rigger

294
00:23:03,201 --> 00:23:08,960
equipped with spiked boots and leg spurs he had the demanding task of ascending and prepping the

295
00:23:08,961 --> 00:23:14,203
spar tree. The high climbers would climb probably 160 to 180 feet up in the air.

296
00:23:14,204 --> 00:23:15,120
Although there

297
00:23:15,121 --> 00:23:19,680
were some spar trees that were taller than that, that's the kind of height that you needed.

298
00:23:20,800 --> 00:23:26,640
At a pre-calculated level, the high rigor faced the daunting task of cutting off the top of the

299
00:23:26,641 --> 00:23:27,341
tree.

300
00:23:27,342 --> 00:23:36,560
And after that tree fell, the weight of that tree would push that spar tree back,

301
00:23:36,561 --> 00:23:39,140
and he would just rock back and forth.

302
00:23:40,680 --> 00:23:42,160
It started to show off.

303
00:23:42,500 --> 00:23:45,740
He'd go up to the top of the tree and sit and have a cigarette.

304
00:23:46,440 --> 00:23:49,360
And if he really had nerves of steel, he'd do a headstand.

305
00:23:50,240 --> 00:23:51,940
With his bar tree topped off,

306
00:23:51,941 --> 00:23:55,820
the high-rigger assisted crewmen in hoisting and securing the high-lead pulley.

307
00:23:57,220 --> 00:24:02,000
As this new breed of daredevils elevated log transport to productive new heights,

308
00:24:02,860 --> 00:24:06,080
locomotive drivers below were literally spinning their wheels.

309
00:24:06,560 --> 00:24:12,220
For a decade, all efforts to haul logs by rail in the mountainous timber country failed miserably.

310
00:24:13,640 --> 00:24:17,080
Locomotives had little or no traction on the steep forest grades.

311
00:24:18,320 --> 00:24:22,600
In 1880, a Michigan lumberman named Ephraim Shea invented the solution.

312
00:24:24,460 --> 00:24:28,120
The Shea locomotive possessed one radical design change.

313
00:24:28,640 --> 00:24:33,700
Power from the lone cylinder was transmitted to the eight wheels, not by rods, but by gears.

314
00:24:33,701 --> 00:24:39,340
Every wheel was locked in a gear, so there was no way a wheel could slip.

315
00:24:39,620 --> 00:24:40,920
It's like a four-wheel drive.

316
00:24:41,360 --> 00:24:47,020
Plus, they were lighter, could navigate grades up to 10 or 12, 14 percent, which was unheard of,

317
00:24:47,280 --> 00:24:49,900
and they could run equally well backward or forward.

318
00:24:51,820 --> 00:24:56,360
Shays enabled loggers to penetrate deeper and deeper into the western forests.

319
00:24:57,340 --> 00:25:00,520
Trainloads of timber rolled from regions once thought inaccessible.

320
00:25:00,521 --> 00:25:08,520
Logs also began moving along the most elaborate and unconventional transport system loggers ever devised, the flume.

321
00:25:08,521 --> 00:25:10,963
It was literally a man-made river.

322
00:25:10,964 --> 00:25:20,520
They built them in 16-foot boxes, as they called them, sections 16 feet long, and hoisted them up on top of trestles in that fashion.

323
00:25:20,521 --> 00:25:26,380
As they start down the real steep areas, the lumber might be going 50 miles an hour, splashing everywhere.

324
00:25:26,680 --> 00:25:32,060
Where there was very little drop in elevation, you could walk along faster than the lumber was floating.

325
00:25:32,940 --> 00:25:37,740
A chronic problem was maintaining the flow of timber along routes that stretched for miles.

326
00:25:39,160 --> 00:25:45,460
A new crew of specialists, flume herders, manned remote outposts to guard against leaks and log jams.

327
00:25:45,461 --> 00:25:51,080
The flume hoarders lived there full time, and they had a bell hanging out over the flume.

328
00:25:51,081 --> 00:25:53,060
So as the lumber went by, it ticked the bell.

329
00:25:53,640 --> 00:25:56,760
So in the middle of the night, if a guy woke up and he didn't hear the bell ticking, he

330
00:25:56,761 --> 00:25:57,620
knew there was a problem.

331
00:25:58,220 --> 00:26:01,920
And he'd jump up, trying to find the source of where the jam hub was.

332
00:26:02,740 --> 00:26:06,920
And if you can imagine, some of these trestles that the flumes were over as they went over

333
00:26:06,921 --> 00:26:10,780
these draws and gullies were 60 to 70 feet up in the air.

334
00:26:11,200 --> 00:26:13,600
These guys were walking on a little tiny catwalk.

335
00:26:13,601 --> 00:26:22,440
That was an extremely dangerous job, and there are many occasions where a flume herder fell off the catwalk to his death.

336
00:26:23,720 --> 00:26:26,040
Beyond the danger, however, was fun.

337
00:26:27,040 --> 00:26:31,340
For years to come, loggers seeking new thrills found them in makeshift flume boats.

338
00:26:33,780 --> 00:26:37,820
Anyone who's been drenched on an amusement park log ride can blame it on them.

339
00:26:37,821 --> 00:26:45,140
By flume, river and rail, logs poured into the sawmills.

340
00:26:45,141 --> 00:26:50,380
The industry's survival depended on the Sawyer's ability to keep pace with the lumberjacks.

341
00:26:50,381 --> 00:26:56,100
Until the 1880s, their most crucial tool was the circular saw.

342
00:26:56,101 --> 00:27:03,060
It was invented in 1777, but now, coupled with steam, its full potential was being harnessed.

343
00:27:03,061 --> 00:27:08,060
Still, the enormous redwood and sequoia logs were often more than they could handle.

344
00:27:08,061 --> 00:27:16,060
In the 1870s, after years of persistence, engineers finally delivered a solution.

345
00:27:16,061 --> 00:27:23,060
They developed the first practical models of an invention patented in 1808, the bandsaw.

346
00:27:23,061 --> 00:27:29,060
For decades, craftsmen had been unable to accurately and securely join the ends of the band.

347
00:27:29,061 --> 00:27:34,080
advances in welding techniques finally resulted in steel loops that could

348
00:27:34,081 --> 00:27:37,980
withstand the enormous pressure exerted by the flywheels above and below the

349
00:27:37,981 --> 00:27:42,460
carriage the fast thin blades could slice unerringly through even the most

350
00:27:42,461 --> 00:27:48,380
gargantuan logs the advancing industry was supplying a rising nation all the

351
00:27:48,381 --> 00:27:53,620
lumber it needed to grow but many look to America's disappearing forests and

352
00:27:53,621 --> 00:27:55,620
and worried that the price was too high.

353
00:27:59,020 --> 00:28:02,920
California's 54-mile Sanger Flume was the longest ever constructed.

354
00:28:03,760 --> 00:28:07,280
It was completed in 1890 at a cost of $300,000,

355
00:28:07,940 --> 00:28:10,820
equivalent to nearly $6 million today.

356
00:28:12,120 --> 00:28:14,240
Logging Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

357
00:28:16,420 --> 00:28:18,960
As the 20th century dawned,

358
00:28:19,140 --> 00:28:22,540
skyrocketing demand and eastern capital were transforming logging

359
00:28:22,541 --> 00:28:25,260
into a colossus of mechanized industry.

360
00:28:26,380 --> 00:28:28,960
A rough and ready trade was absorbed by corporate America

361
00:28:28,961 --> 00:28:30,740
and the timber barons.

362
00:28:32,380 --> 00:28:35,020
The greatest of them all was Frederick Weyerhaeuser,

363
00:28:35,500 --> 00:28:37,880
a German immigrant who began as a night watchman

364
00:28:37,881 --> 00:28:39,220
in an Illinois sawmill.

365
00:28:40,420 --> 00:28:43,140
In 1858, when the mill hit hard times,

366
00:28:44,100 --> 00:28:46,480
Weyerhaeuser scraped together enough money to buy it.

367
00:28:47,340 --> 00:28:48,540
In the decades that followed,

368
00:28:49,040 --> 00:28:51,140
he built up the most powerful logging company

369
00:28:51,141 --> 00:28:55,936
surrounding the Great Lakes. Then he turned west.

370
00:28:55,937 --> 00:28:59,460
In 1900, Weyerhaeuser bought 900,000

371
00:28:59,461 --> 00:29:04,299
acres of prime Washington timberland from the Northern Pacific Railroad.

372
00:29:04,300 --> 00:29:05,240
The price tag,

373
00:29:05,680 --> 00:29:11,600
$5.4 million, to that time the largest private land transaction in American history.

374
00:29:13,300 --> 00:29:17,780
As the Weyerhaeusers of the West took control of the industry, there was still room for

375
00:29:17,781 --> 00:29:23,720
Maverick. Simon Benson was a former bullwhacker who built his own thriving

376
00:29:23,721 --> 00:29:29,120
company in Washington. In 1906 the enterprising Benson built a sawmill in

377
00:29:29,121 --> 00:29:33,920
San Diego. His master plan was to satisfy all the lumber needs of a growing

378
00:29:33,921 --> 00:29:38,840
Southern California. But shipping his logs from Washington by rail would spell

379
00:29:38,841 --> 00:29:46,300
bankruptcy. Benson's solution was as daring as it was ingenious. Like a 20th

380
00:29:46,301 --> 00:29:52,060
century Noah he would mass his logs to construct enormous ocean-going rafts

381
00:29:52,061 --> 00:29:57,400
what he designed was a timber frame and this thing was almost a thousand feet

382
00:29:57,401 --> 00:30:02,320
long and the logs came in from the woods he would take this big derrick and it

383
00:30:02,321 --> 00:30:06,880
would load these logs into this wooden frame and he was very very careful of

384
00:30:06,881 --> 00:30:11,680
overlapping the joints of the logs so he didn't have all the ends at one place

385
00:30:11,681 --> 00:30:14,120
He'd overlap them to give them some strength.

386
00:30:14,720 --> 00:30:19,000
And when they were finally finished, they would take chains and wrap them around.

387
00:30:19,360 --> 00:30:20,800
And these chains are huge.

388
00:30:20,880 --> 00:30:24,480
These are those anchor chains that you see, absolutely massive.

389
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:28,960
The chains alone weighed 250 tons.

390
00:30:30,340 --> 00:30:34,800
Released from their timber frames, Benson's rafts were as seaworthy as battleships.

391
00:30:35,700 --> 00:30:38,800
Many contained enough lumber to build 500 houses.

392
00:30:38,801 --> 00:30:45,920
over the next 40 years close to 120 navigated the 1100 mile voyage to San Diego

393
00:30:45,921 --> 00:30:53,740
innovators like Benson pushed productivity to levels once thought unimaginable loggers cut a

394
00:30:53,741 --> 00:31:00,280
swath through the western timberlands as if forests were an inexhaustible resource at many sites they

395
00:31:00,281 --> 00:31:06,640
literally stripped the landscape then at the turn of the 20th century the shriek of the donkey engine

396
00:31:06,641 --> 00:31:09,575
was challenged by the voice of protest.

397
00:31:09,576 --> 00:31:13,640
Naturalists like John Muir deemed the frontier forests

398
00:31:14,180 --> 00:31:16,980
a living treasure that required protection.

399
00:31:16,981 --> 00:31:20,040
Muir's greatest champion was President Theodore

400
00:31:20,041 --> 00:31:20,741
Roosevelt.

401
00:31:20,742 --> 00:31:25,820
As both nature lover and die-hard capitalist, Roosevelt declared that America

402
00:31:25,821 --> 00:31:31,440
had to balance its demand for lumber with a keener sensitivity to the environment.

403
00:31:31,441 --> 00:31:37,260
In 1905, he created the United States Forest Service and appointed renowned conservationist

404
00:31:37,261 --> 00:31:40,200
Gifford Pinchot as its head.

405
00:31:40,201 --> 00:31:44,340
Pinchot believed America's national forests could be managed best by combining a reverence

406
00:31:44,341 --> 00:31:48,660
for nature with responsible exploitation.

407
00:31:48,661 --> 00:31:54,280
The timber barons on the one side felt that he was too excessive and too restrictive in

408
00:31:54,281 --> 00:31:56,640
terms of how forestry should be practiced.

409
00:31:56,641 --> 00:32:01,360
The John Muir's on the other hand felt that nobody should be using forest period.

410
00:32:01,361 --> 00:32:03,688
issues he set aside and left alone.

411
00:32:03,689 --> 00:32:07,280
Gifford Pinchot was a person who said look we can have

412
00:32:07,281 --> 00:32:09,902
our cake and we can eat it too.

413
00:32:09,903 --> 00:32:15,400
Pinchot's policy of multiple use set aside some forest land to be

414
00:32:15,401 --> 00:32:19,079
preserved while opening up other regions for lumbermen.

415
00:32:19,080 --> 00:32:21,220
A century after its inception it

416
00:32:21,221 --> 00:32:23,287
still provokes controversy.

417
00:32:23,288 --> 00:32:27,880
Some believe it puts a stranglehold on the logging industry.

418
00:32:28,860 --> 00:32:31,980
Others think it leaves America's forests too vulnerable.

419
00:32:34,700 --> 00:32:40,960
Beginning in 1926, the debate intensified as the woods echoed with the roar of a new invention.

420
00:32:47,620 --> 00:32:53,460
The first practical chainsaws were the brainchild of mechanical engineer Andreas Still.

421
00:32:54,420 --> 00:32:59,620
His key design element was a chain of cutting teeth that rotated in a groove around a bar.

422
00:33:00,520 --> 00:33:03,840
The blades resembled the simple straight teeth of a crosscut saw.

423
00:33:04,920 --> 00:33:11,240
Until the 1940s, however, his prototypes weighed close to 150 pounds and required a two-man crew.

424
00:33:12,420 --> 00:33:16,120
The heavy gasoline engines had clutches that had to be engaged manually.

425
00:33:18,020 --> 00:33:19,640
Later models increased efficiency.

426
00:33:20,500 --> 00:33:24,940
Then in 1946, a logger and inventor named Buford Cox

427
00:33:24,941 --> 00:33:27,860
conceived a radical new design for the sawteeth.

428
00:33:28,720 --> 00:33:30,940
He was sitting at the wood pile one day,

429
00:33:31,320 --> 00:33:33,980
and he was looking at this big wood-boring insect.

430
00:33:34,560 --> 00:33:36,220
And they're about as big as your finger.

431
00:33:36,800 --> 00:33:39,640
And what they have is they have big mandible jaws in front.

432
00:33:40,020 --> 00:33:42,580
And they swipe at the timber and the wood

433
00:33:42,581 --> 00:33:47,300
and make this nice oval-shaped hole that they would bore through.

434
00:33:47,301 --> 00:33:51,020
And he looked at that and he thought, you know, if they can do that,

435
00:33:51,340 --> 00:33:53,060
I wonder if we could do that with the chainsaw.

436
00:33:53,660 --> 00:33:57,720
What Beaufort Cox did is he actually took that tooth and curled it over.

437
00:33:59,040 --> 00:34:02,800
And that corner of that tooth would grab the wood and pull it out.

438
00:34:04,180 --> 00:34:08,980
And then that really was the start of modern-day chain for the chainsaws.

439
00:34:10,060 --> 00:34:11,660
The chain became better.

440
00:34:12,000 --> 00:34:13,280
The engines became lighter.

441
00:34:13,920 --> 00:34:16,320
We had better technology in the weight.

442
00:34:16,321 --> 00:34:22,660
We did a better job of the exhaust gases, for example, moving them away from the operator.

443
00:34:23,240 --> 00:34:27,080
We had the upper hand, if you will, once the chainsaw came into the woods,

444
00:34:27,240 --> 00:34:29,940
because we could really cut the trees fast.

445
00:34:31,220 --> 00:34:34,120
Today's chainsaws are driven by a centrifugal clutch

446
00:34:34,121 --> 00:34:40,220
and can revolve at up to 13,000 revolutions per minute.

447
00:34:40,780 --> 00:34:44,060
As they've revolutionized the industry over the past 60 years,

448
00:34:44,061 --> 00:34:47,220
So has another bold idea from the 1940s.

449
00:34:47,880 --> 00:34:53,780
Before World War II, foresters began planting seedlings in the barren landscapes, clear-cut by lumberjacks.

450
00:34:54,500 --> 00:34:59,780
The new generation of trees was specifically intended to be harvested decades later as timber.

451
00:35:00,880 --> 00:35:03,200
It marked the birth of the tree farm.

452
00:35:05,780 --> 00:35:12,440
Since then, 37,000 tree farms have sprouted across the United States, most of them in the South.

453
00:35:14,060 --> 00:35:20,180
Seedlings are first planted and cultivated in nurseries, then transplanted in harvesting

454
00:35:20,181 --> 00:35:21,180
tracts.

455
00:35:21,181 --> 00:35:26,440
They can grow trees much like you grow corn.

456
00:35:26,441 --> 00:35:27,680
Trees are planted in rows.

457
00:35:27,681 --> 00:35:31,300
You can manage them because you can put equipment on the flat terrain.

458
00:35:31,301 --> 00:35:38,680
So there are some advantages to being able to farm the tree as a crop.

459
00:35:38,681 --> 00:35:43,600
Today, more than a third of the world's harvested timber comes from tree farms.

460
00:35:44,800 --> 00:35:50,500
Some experts predict that by the year 2040, they will cover an expanse the size of Utah.

461
00:35:51,780 --> 00:35:56,280
Their expansion may seem the most obvious solution to the nation's rising demand for wood,

462
00:35:56,820 --> 00:36:00,420
but obvious solutions are often not as simple as they first appear.

463
00:36:01,980 --> 00:36:06,660
A tree farm is not a forest. A forest is a web of life with many different species,

464
00:36:06,661 --> 00:36:14,680
a great variety of plants, and everything all interacting together to have literally a web of life.

465
00:36:15,060 --> 00:36:20,380
When you change the forest to a single crop of trees or even a few species of trees,

466
00:36:20,740 --> 00:36:23,660
the result is a tree plantation that is very sterile,

467
00:36:24,320 --> 00:36:30,300
rather than there being the diversity that is essential for species to survive in the long term.

468
00:36:30,301 --> 00:36:37,300
Despite such concerns, many see few viable alternatives as we look to the future.

469
00:36:37,301 --> 00:36:45,300
We're going to grow from 300 million people to a billion people one day in the United States,

470
00:36:45,301 --> 00:36:47,300
and wood has to come from someplace.

471
00:36:47,301 --> 00:36:54,300
So I suspect that we may have to have areas of the United States where wood fiber has grown on very short rotations

472
00:36:54,301 --> 00:37:00,640
so that we can reduce our costs and yet provide a lot of wood for America's needs.

473
00:37:01,940 --> 00:37:07,260
The debate is just part of an enduring conflict between the logging industry and environmentalists.

474
00:37:08,460 --> 00:37:11,820
Their struggle is the result of a society at cross purposes,

475
00:37:12,520 --> 00:37:16,340
one that seeks to protect its resources as much as exploit them.

476
00:37:18,060 --> 00:37:23,900
In an effort to solve the dilemma, innovators are literally taking bold new steps.

477
00:37:24,300 --> 00:37:32,620
The 13 southern states produce 60% of America's wood, almost four times the current volume

478
00:37:32,621 --> 00:37:35,920
of the Pacific Northwest.

479
00:37:35,921 --> 00:37:39,980
Logging Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

480
00:37:39,981 --> 00:37:46,380
Today's logging industry faces obstacles Paul Bunyan might have found insurmountable.

481
00:37:46,381 --> 00:37:53,400
It must feed a wood-hungry world that has a passion to preserve its forests.

482
00:37:53,401 --> 00:37:59,120
must meet the spiraling demands of a new century, yet avoid the practices that scarred the last.

483
00:38:00,220 --> 00:38:04,080
Three centuries of exploitation have cut deeply into the American landscape

484
00:38:04,081 --> 00:38:09,520
and into the federally owned forests that comprise 29% of the nation's timberlands.

485
00:38:10,040 --> 00:38:15,560
In the Sierra Nevada's National Forest, all the way through until the early 1990s,

486
00:38:15,561 --> 00:38:18,000
clear-cutting was the main method of harvest.

487
00:38:18,001 --> 00:38:23,760
And so what we end up with is a site that is a bare, denuded hillside,

488
00:38:23,980 --> 00:38:29,660
and the values that are important for wildlife and other species are no longer there.

489
00:38:29,860 --> 00:38:34,860
It's just putting human needs first, the human needs of growing a crop of wood.

490
00:38:36,540 --> 00:38:39,460
In their zeal to satisfy the nation's demands,

491
00:38:40,120 --> 00:38:43,280
loggers destroyed much of nature's most monumental handiwork.

492
00:38:43,281 --> 00:38:48,280
less than 4% of America's old growth timber remains.

493
00:38:50,280 --> 00:38:53,280
In the matter of the sequoia trees in Converse Basin

494
00:38:53,281 --> 00:38:56,280
that was logged by the Sanger Lumber Company,

495
00:38:56,281 --> 00:38:59,280
which I call the most destructive logging in the history of the world,

496
00:38:59,281 --> 00:39:02,280
they cut down 8,000 giant sequoias,

497
00:39:02,281 --> 00:39:06,280
and those trees take 2,000, 3,000, 4,000 years to grow big.

498
00:39:07,280 --> 00:39:11,280
Destruction on a scale far greater is now occurring in South America,

499
00:39:11,281 --> 00:39:15,820
where 200,000 square miles of the Amazon rainforest have fallen.

500
00:39:17,460 --> 00:39:20,600
There, few government controls exist to prevent developers

501
00:39:20,601 --> 00:39:23,580
from stripping the landscape of its rich diversity of wildlife.

502
00:39:26,040 --> 00:39:30,940
In the U.S., loggers operate according to strict federal and state regulations,

503
00:39:31,500 --> 00:39:34,420
but they continue to cross swords with environmentalists,

504
00:39:35,020 --> 00:39:39,400
as each asserts their own visions of what constitutes responsible forest management.

505
00:39:39,401 --> 00:39:46,220
Forest issues are not black and white, and obviously the vast majority of foresters and

506
00:39:46,221 --> 00:39:52,780
those trying to produce timber on forest lands do care about wildlife and water and other

507
00:39:52,781 --> 00:39:58,580
values, and the vast majority of environmentalists recognize that you have to produce wood products.

508
00:39:58,581 --> 00:40:05,880
So in many ways the challenge is for the environmentalists and loggers to work more and more toward

509
00:40:05,881 --> 00:40:10,520
the common middle ground where there are a lot of overlapping opportunities.

510
00:40:13,400 --> 00:40:20,440
In my grandfather's era after World War II, it was production, you know, get the pieces to the

511
00:40:20,441 --> 00:40:27,400
roadside, get them to the mill, and you know brute force and strength, and today it's more finesse and

512
00:40:27,401 --> 00:40:32,060
and minimal disturbance to the environment.

513
00:40:33,600 --> 00:40:36,320
Today, we have to get production, but we have to do it smart.

514
00:40:37,760 --> 00:40:40,860
These days, there's a lot of new high-tech equipment

515
00:40:40,861 --> 00:40:42,760
that's being used in the timber industry,

516
00:40:43,060 --> 00:40:46,680
and our center is very supportive of the conversion

517
00:40:46,681 --> 00:40:49,760
to this high-tech equipment because it benefits the environment.

518
00:40:50,820 --> 00:40:56,300
The cut-to-length processors have a minimal pressure per square inch,

519
00:40:56,301 --> 00:40:59,900
and so they don't compact the soil like traditional equipment does.

520
00:41:00,120 --> 00:41:01,740
They don't create huge ruts.

521
00:41:02,220 --> 00:41:05,700
They're very, very light, even on moderately steep hillsides,

522
00:41:05,840 --> 00:41:07,600
very, very light on the land.

523
00:41:09,060 --> 00:41:10,960
The less compacted the soil,

524
00:41:11,100 --> 00:41:13,360
the better the chances for the trees left standing.

525
00:41:15,360 --> 00:41:19,160
Since the 1970s, loggers have demonstrated this most dramatically

526
00:41:19,161 --> 00:41:20,280
with the helicopter.

527
00:41:21,400 --> 00:41:25,320
Nothing protects the fragile forest floor more than rising above it.

528
00:41:26,300 --> 00:41:31,460
But airborne operations are rare, conducted mainly in remote areas inaccessible to ground machinery.

529
00:41:33,960 --> 00:41:38,420
Will those types of machines become more important in the present and into the future?

530
00:41:38,560 --> 00:41:41,260
Probably so, but we're going to have to reduce their cost.

531
00:41:42,200 --> 00:41:43,140
They're very expensive.

532
00:41:43,620 --> 00:41:46,880
I think they're about seven times more expensive than conventional systems.

533
00:41:50,260 --> 00:41:53,700
Scandinavian engineers are developing what may be a feasible alternative.

534
00:41:53,701 --> 00:42:00,700
Their six-legged prototypes, aptly called walking machines, can negotiate even the steepest terrain.

535
00:42:00,701 --> 00:42:06,700
Their impact on productivity could be as profound as their lack of impact on the forest floor.

536
00:42:06,701 --> 00:42:10,700
That technology is very intriguing.

537
00:42:10,701 --> 00:42:13,700
You're just putting that weight on one spot.

538
00:42:13,701 --> 00:42:21,700
You don't see the impact of like a road through the trees that you do with using wheeled equipment that creates a path.

539
00:42:21,701 --> 00:42:27,700
It was developed in the Nordic countries when they were trying to harvest areas that were peaked bogs,

540
00:42:27,701 --> 00:42:29,700
and they couldn't hold up the heavy equipment.

541
00:42:29,701 --> 00:42:33,700
And it senses when it won't have enough support for it

542
00:42:33,701 --> 00:42:39,700
and will put the actual foot of the machine in a different place once it knows it's not going to support it.

543
00:42:41,700 --> 00:42:45,700
Can technology help bridge the gap between the logging industry and environmentalists?

544
00:42:45,701 --> 00:42:47,700
Both camps are hopeful.

545
00:42:47,701 --> 00:42:53,340
are hopeful, but their enduring struggle seems destined to escalate at the same rate as our

546
00:42:53,341 --> 00:42:55,398
global appetite for wood.

547
00:42:55,399 --> 00:43:00,420
Policymakers will have to balance our needs as consumers with

548
00:43:00,421 --> 00:43:03,140
our concerns for the health of our forests.

549
00:43:03,141 --> 00:43:05,497
Right now, it's at a stalemate.

550
00:43:05,498 --> 00:43:09,680
And until we have some charismatic leaders come forward

551
00:43:09,681 --> 00:43:14,940
from the environmental community and from the timber industry and the agencies that

552
00:43:14,941 --> 00:43:18,780
manage our public lands, we're not going to have a resolution of the conflict.

553
00:43:19,580 --> 00:43:24,460
Most environmentalists recognize that we all need to use wood and they don't want to say

554
00:43:24,461 --> 00:43:29,578
stop logging even though there will be a few that have that extreme view.

555
00:43:29,579 --> 00:43:31,260
But the vast majority of

556
00:43:31,261 --> 00:43:37,500
environmental groups recognize that responsible forest management can provide wood products that

557
00:43:37,501 --> 00:43:44,247
we all need. We need to be able to go to the lumber yard and buy two by four.

558
00:43:44,248 --> 00:43:44,948
We need

559
00:43:44,949 --> 00:43:46,960
We need to be able to put up houses.

560
00:43:46,961 --> 00:43:49,660
But we don't need to clear cut the way we used to.

561
00:43:49,661 --> 00:43:53,500
We don't need to destroy the wilderness the way we used to.

562
00:43:53,501 --> 00:43:55,240
So we need some moderation.

563
00:43:55,241 --> 00:43:57,160
We need government control.

564
00:43:57,161 --> 00:44:01,260
John Muir said that Uncle Sam was the only one who could preserve the forest,

565
00:44:01,261 --> 00:44:03,860
and he was absolutely right.

566
00:44:03,861 --> 00:44:06,560
If the past is prologue,

567
00:44:06,561 --> 00:44:12,400
loggers will continue to meet our insatiable demand for wood with imagination and innovation.

568
00:44:12,401 --> 00:44:16,200
In a complex world that strives to revere nature as well as exploit it,

569
00:44:16,740 --> 00:44:19,440
their challenge will be to defy the popular saying

570
00:44:20,880 --> 00:44:23,520
and always see the forest for the trees.

571
00:44:25,580 --> 00:44:29,360
The way to here, here, and here goes through here.

572
00:44:29,660 --> 00:44:32,080
The Lumberyard, where big things start.

573
00:44:32,860 --> 00:44:35,320
Floating, cutting, shaping, and chipping.

574
00:44:35,560 --> 00:44:40,000
In this vast, noisy complex, the mighty tools don't take breaks.

575
00:44:40,001 --> 00:44:44,040
The Lumberyard on Modern Marbles, next on the History Channel.

